# 5.8.2
- `localSuppression()` / `kAnon()` gain a `method` argument. The default
  `"heuristic"` is the original sweep and is unchanged, cell for cell. The new
  engines decide *which* cells to suppress differently and never need more
  than the sweep on the benchmark files:
  + `"greedy2"`: cost-effectiveness greedy with exact per-record steps, no
    solver required (Rcpp core, `src/greedy2.cpp`)
  + `"optimal"`: exact MILP over the violator-only restriction
  + `"aggregate"`: exact MILP indexed by distinct tuples, for the unrestricted
    problem; also takes any `alpha`, per-record thresholds and classical
    (identical-tuple) k-anonymity
  + `"lns"`: large-neighbourhood search around the pair-based model
  The MILP engines need a backend: `highs` (Suggests, CRAN) is enough; `scip`
  (Suggests, CRAN) and `gurobi` (Suggests; not on CRAN, available from
  gurobi.com under its own licence, as e.g. `prioritizr` declares it) are
  alternatives. A `control`
  list carries `solver`, `time_limit` and `max_per_record`. Stratification,
  `combs` and ghost variables work with every engine because the dispatch sits
  in `suppSubset()`, below all of them.
- `sdcApp` exposes the engines. The k-anonymity panel gains an algorithm
  selector that offers only what can run on the current problem (a MILP
  backend must be installed for the exact engines; every engine but
  `"aggregate"` needs `alpha = 1`), the solver, time limit and per-record cap
  for the exact engines, and a line stating how many records still violate the
  selected k. The generated script, the summary page and the SDC report record
  which engine ran and what it proved -- an audit trail must show that a solve
  was truncated, not only that it was started.
- A suppression pattern is never handed on unverified: every engine's result is
  re-counted with `freqCalc()`'s own count at the problem's `alpha` before it
  is returned. A solver that finds no solution within its time limit, or a
  per-record cap that makes an instance infeasible, now raises an error instead
  of returning a partially protected file.
- New exported function `ls_solvers()`: which MILP backends are installed.
- New exported functions behind those engines: `ls_check()` (the criterion as
  `freqCalc()` counts it, plus classical k-anonymity), `ls_greedy2()`,
  `ls_optimal()`, `ls_aggregate()`, `ls_lns()`, `ls_hub()` (the closed-form
  construction), `ls_minuc()`, `ls_target()` (per-record thresholds from an
  individual-risk bound), `ls_supp_subset()` and `solve_milp()`.
- New tests (2,200 lines) including a brute-force oracle over all suppression
  patterns that every exact engine is checked against.
- Documentation of a usage pitfall: `freqCalc()` coerces key columns with
  `as.numeric()`, so *character* keys are read as missing, i.e. as wildcards,
  and `kAnon()` then reports success while suppressing nothing. Recode to
  factor or integer first; `ls_check()` and `ls_target()` do so internally.
- `AI_applyAnonymization()` gains early stopping of the refinement loop via the new
  `tol` and `patience` arguments: refinement now terminates once the combined utility
  score U stops improving by more than `tol` for `patience` consecutive iterations,
  instead of always running all `max_iter` rounds.
- `query_llm()` now omits the `temperature` parameter for OpenAI GPT-5.x and o-series
  reasoning models (which do not accept a custom temperature), enabling use of e.g. `gpt-5.5`.
- Bugfix: `ldiversity()` now computes distinct l-diversity measure correctly in case of `NAs` in Keyvars
  * improved/simplified uderlying C++ code
  * added Unit-Tests for `ldiversity()`
- Fixing some Header-Definitions for CRAN-Compliance
- Updated Unit-Tests for `pram()`

# 5.8.1
- New AI-assisted anonymization features:
  + `AI_createSdcObj()`: LLM-assisted variable classification into SDC roles
    (key variables, numerical variables, PRAM variables, weight, household ID)
  + `AI_applyAnonymization()`: Agentic loop that proposes, evaluates, and refines
    anonymization strategies using structured tool calling
  + Provider-agnostic LLM access via `query_llm()` supporting OpenAI, Anthropic,
    and any OpenAI-compatible endpoint (Ollama, Azure, vLLM)
  + Privacy by design: only metadata (variable names, types, cardinality, factor
    levels) is sent to the LLM — never the actual microdata
  + Combined utility score for quantitative strategy comparison
  + New vignette: "AI-Assisted Statistical Disclosure Control with sdcMicro"
- AI integration in `sdcApp()`:
  + New "AI-Assisted" tab for interactive strategy generation and comparison
  + "AI suggest variables" button in the setup panel with auto-fill
  + Applied AI methods tracked in the Reproducibility tab
- Renamed function prefixes from `KI_` to `AI_` for consistency
- Remove `shinyBS` dependency; replaced with pure R+JS implementations
- Fix deprecated enum-to-float arithmetic warnings in C++ code (`RankSwapping.h`, `Mdav.h`) flagged by clang 21+ and gcc 14+

